activation	Print prompt	2026-10-28T04:11:14.0027765Z {"title": "`EXPLAIN MERGE ... RETURNING` segfaults when the optimizer is disabled", "body": "### What happens?\n\n`EXPLAIN` on a `MERGE ... RETURNING` statement terminates the DuckDB process with SIGSEGV when `PRAGMA disable_optimizer` is set. The same `MERGE ... RETURNING` executes successfully; `EXPLAIN` also works if the optimizer is enabled or `RETURNING` is removed.\n\n### To Reproduce\n\nRun the following SQL in a fresh DuckDB process. No rows or external data are needed.\n\n```sql\nPRAGMA disable_optimizer;\nCREATE TABLE t(i INTEGER);\nEXPLAIN MERGE INTO t\nUSING (SELECT 1 AS i) AS s ON t.i=s.i\nWHEN MATCHED THEN DELETE\nRETURNING i;\n```\n\nObserved process status:\n\n```text\nSegmentation fault (SIGSEGV); exit code 139\n```\n\nExpected: `EXPLAIN` should return a plan or a normal SQL error without terminating the process. In fresh processes, the following controls all complete successfully: optimizer enabled with the same `EXPLAIN MERGE ... RETURNING`; optimizer disabled with `EXPLAIN MERGE` without `RETURNING`; optimizer disabled with direct execution of `MERGE ... RETURNING`. `EXPLAIN DELETE`, `UPDATE`, and `INSERT` with `RETURNING` also work with the optimizer disabled.\n\n### OS:\n\nDebian GNU/Linux 12 (bookworm), x86_64, in a fresh Docker container for the 1.5.6 replay.\n\n### DuckDB Version:\n\n1.5.6, installed using `uv` from `duckdb==1.5.6` in the clean Python 3.13 container. Also reproduced on 1.4.5, 1.5.4, 1.5.5 (official DuckDB CLI images), and the local 2.0 preview package `2.0.0.dev2609250715`.\n\n### DuckDB Client:\n\nPython API on 1.5.6 and the 2.0 preview; official CLI on 1.4.5\u20131.5.5. The CLI reproduction on 1.5.5 also exits 139.\n\n### Hardware:\n\n_No response_\n\n### Full Name:\n\nWenjie Dong\n\n### Affiliation:\n\nNone\n\n### Did you include all relevant configuration (e.g., CPU architecture, Linux distribution) to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [x] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all code required to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [x] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all relevant data sets for reproducing the issue?\n\nNot applicable - the reproduction does not require a data set"}
